description:
In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/feature-compose.ts, rank a VERIFIED-unique region as the strongest anchor locator instead of the weakest.

ANCHOR. This exact line occurs EXACTLY ONCE in the file:
            const anchorLocators = [

ONE OPERATION. Insert the following lines IMMEDIATELY AFTER that line, before the existing `...regionCandidatesFromText(` element:

              // A region VERIFIED unique in this file is the STRONGEST locator, not the weakest.
              // The note below is correct about a MINED grounding term, whose first occurrence is
              // routinely a doc comment far from the code it names. It is not correct about a
              // region that was produced by checking the deployed file: that is a fact about the
              // file, so hoist it. Gated on occurring exactly once, so a legacy mined region that
              // is non-unique keeps the old ordering and still appears last.
              ...((regionHint && text.split(regionHint).length - 1 === 1) ? [regionHint] : []),

Change nothing else. Leave the existing `regionHint ?? "",` entry at the end of the array in place — a duplicate is harmless because renderSafeAnchors tries locators in order and the first usable match wins. Do not modify the comment block above the array, the regionCandidatesFromText element, focusHints, or any import.

WHY. `anchorLocators` currently orders: mined candidates from the gap text first, then focusHints, then `regionHint` LAST. The comment above it explains that choice, and it is sound for the region source it was written against — a mined grounding term. But regions are now produced by verifying a literal occurs exactly once in the deployed file under /vessels, which makes them the most reliable locator available, and they are currently ranked below as many as 59 mined candidates.

MEASURED. Region coverage on open gaps went from 0.6% to ~13% today via a producer that only writes a literal after confirming it occurs exactly once in the deployed file. The `[fc-scope] region-named gap` signal confirms those regions reach the pipeline. But the dominant current compose failure is `no_unique_anchor: refused fs_edit — planned anchor is non-unique and re-derivation found no unique substring`, which is the drafter selecting a non-unique old_string while a verified-unique locator sits last in the list it is offered. Today's recommit census: semantic_reject 25.5%, compose_execution_failure 19.6% (dominated by that no_unique_anchor refusal), syntax_break 15.7%, anchor_not_found 15.7%.

`text` is already in scope here — it is passed to renderSafeAnchors on the line a few below. The uniqueness test is a plain string count on the file contents already in hand, so this adds no I/O.

